<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">often dated to <a href="/years/1746">1746</a> and linked with the Jacobite rising of <a href="/years/1745">1745</a>; printed by <a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond&quot;, or simply &quot;Loch Lomond&quot;, is a traditional Scottish folk song (Roud 9598), named after the largest loch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>; in Scots &quot;bonnie&quot; means fair or beautiful. Its maker is unknown. It has often been dated to 1746, and its origins are thought to lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a>, with which it has long been associated. Two readings of the words are common.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In the best-known words the singer recalls the banks where he and his true love walked, and the chorus answers: &quot;O ye'll tak' the high road, and I'll tak' the low road, And I'll be in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> afore ye&quot;, for the lovers will never meet again. One reading ties the song to December 1745, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Bonnie Prince Charlie</a> retreated to Scotland, and hears a soldier rueing his sacrifice for the king; in Celtic mythology the low road was a fairy route by which a soldier killed abroad returned home in spirit.</p><p>The other reading makes the chorus the last words of a Jacobite rebel before his execution, perhaps at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a>, where dozens of rebels were executed: he sees his lover at the gallows and tells her they will meet again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> by different routes. The song later became, despite these Jacobite readings, one of a handful of folk signifiers of British unity as the empire grew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>One of the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printings</a> of the music was made in 1840 by Paterson and Sons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, titled &quot;Bonnie Loch Loman&quot; and credited to &quot;a lady&quot;, with arrangements by Finlay Dunn and John Thomson. Lady John Scott was often named as the composer, but she only wrote down the tune and words after hearing a boy sing them in the streets of Edinburgh. Precedents for the tune have been found in the Danish and Faroese &quot;Dankonning lod gribe en havfrue fin&quot; and in &quot;Godesses&quot;, a tune in John Playford's 1651 collection The Dancing Master.</p><p>Both the 1840 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and Lady John Scott's words differ markedly from the common form of today, while an 1849 broadside, &quot;Flora's Lament for Her Charlie&quot;, largely parallels it. The song is often wrongly credited to the poet Andrew Lang, who in 1876 wrote his own poem &quot;The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond, 1746&quot;. The Irish song &quot;Red Is the Rose&quot;, popularised by Tommy Makem, is sung to the same melody with similar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Ralph Vaughan Williams arranged the song for baritone and male choir in 1921, and Charles Ives quoted it in &quot;The Alcotts&quot; movement of his Concord Sonata. The jazz singer Maxine Sullivan recorded it on 6 August 1937 in an arrangement by Claude Thornhill, and her swing version led other acts to swing folk songs. The Scottish band Runrig released it as their first single in 1982 and recorded it again in 2007 with 50,000 supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> national football team for the BBC's Children in Need appeal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</b>&quot;, or simply &quot;Loch Lomond&quot;, is a traditional Scottish folk song (Roud 9598), named after the largest loch in Scotland; in Scots &quot;bonnie&quot; means fair or beautiful.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a></li></ul></section>
